# {{crate_name}}

F Prime sequences for this deployment, compiled to WebAssembly and run on board by
[`Svc::WasmSequencer`](https://github.com/nasa/fprime).

## Building

```shell
rustup target add {{target}}
cargo build --release
```

The target comes from `.cargo/config.toml`, so `--target` is not needed. Each
sequence links to its own module under `target/{{target}}/release/`, for example
`{{sequence}}.wasm`.

## Adding a sequence

```shell
fprime-wasm add <name>
```

That writes `src/bin/<name>.rs` and declares the `[[bin]]` it needs. Doing it by
hand means remembering `test = false` and `bench = false`, without which Cargo
tries to build a test harness for a `#![no_main]` binary.

## Verifying a sequence

```shell
fprime-wasm verify
```

Runs each module on a `spacewasm` interpreter with the same host interface and the
same limits as the on-board one, and reports what it needs: guest memory, guest
stack, interpreter heap pages, code pages, operand stack. Those map directly onto
`Svc::WasmSequencer::Config`, so the output is what you configure the component
with.

## The dictionary

`{{dictionary}}` is this deployment's dictionary, and `build.rs` turns it into the
typed API the sequences call. Replace it from a newer deployment build and the
next `cargo build` picks it up; if a command or channel was renamed or removed,
the sequences that used it stop compiling, which is the point.

## Dependencies

`fprime_core` and `fprime_build` are pinned to {{dependency}}.
